# Stop integrity

The failure this kills: ending a turn with open work while narrating an
external excuse — "ran out of runway/context/time" — a chosen stop
dressed as an event. Text promises demonstrably do not bind; the
mechanical layer is `STOP-DECLARED` in `10-rule-enforcement.mdc`.

## The stop classes — the only valid stops

Ending a turn while open work exists (pending todos, or a `Driver: <sid>`
doc with open rows) requires one `STOP:` line in
`.unlazy/gate-evidence/<sid>.md`:

| Class | Means | The line must carry |
|---|---|---|
| `STOP: USER-GATE — <question>` | only the user can decide | the exact question put to the user — **substance-checked**: a bare label (fewer than 4 words) is rejected; the assistant output the runtime reports (`last_assistant_message`) must actually contain a question |
| `STOP: BLOCKED — <blocker + evidence>` | a named external blocker | the failing command / error text / artifact path — **path-checked**: a cited path must exist; **command-checked** (when the runtime supplies a transcript): a backticked command must appear in the session transcript |
| `STOP: ACCEPTED — <agreement>` | the user agreed to pause | what was agreed and when — **verified**: the prompt-scan must have recorded a user pause/assent signal this session |

That is the whole allowlist. There is deliberately **no capacity
class**: the runtime owns halting — compaction continues the work
automatically — so "ran out of tokens/context/time/runway" can never be
a reason to end a turn. If the runtime halts mid-work, no declaration
is owed (the halt is the event); a voluntary end is always a choice,
and the choice names its real basis. If no class fits, keep working or
ask the user.

`EXEMPT-STOP: * — <reason>` covers the honest odd case (a question-only
turn riding a stale backlog list) — an auditable assertion like every
other exemption, not a silent skip.

## The deferral discharges — speech-side, gate-enforced

`STOP:` classes govern tracked open work. `PUSHBACK` governs the other
half: the final message's own deferral vocabulary — items named in
prose that never reached a ledger. Every deferral-flagged sentence in
the closing message exits one of three ways, each corroborated rather
than asserted:

| Discharge line (in gate-evidence) | When legal | The corroboration |
|---|---|---|
| `ACCEPTED-DEFERRAL: <item> — user said "<verbatim>"` | the user agreed this item waits | the quoted text must appear verbatim in a user turn this session — a fabricated quote is a caught lie, not a discharge |
| `LANE: user said "<verbatim>"` | another agent/session provably owns the surface | same verbatim-user-turn check — "plum's lane" is legal only when the user actually assigned it |
| `IMPOSSIBLE: <wall> — tried: <paths/commands>` | the capability genuinely doesn't exist | for known capabilities (the staff-session minter, headless browsers, the local app), the tried-set must include a backticked command naming the known path that the transcript shows actually ran — "tried" means ran, not named |

A wall-shaped deferral already covered by a `STOP: BLOCKED` /
completion-matrix `BLOCKED:` row with shared content words also
discharges — the same item, one declaration.

The impossibility subclass is `CAPABILITY-LIE`: "can't verify — needs
a live authed desk" while `.unlazy/mint-staff-session.py` sits in the
repo is not a wall, it is a deferral wearing a wall. The check
cross-references the claim's vocabulary against tools the repo
provably holds (session minters — discovered dynamically under
`.unlazy/` and `.scratch/`, not a hardcoded list — playwright/puppeteer,
the local server, the dev DB) — existence refutes the claim class.

The same scan runs over **every markdown doc this session wrote**
(tracked write-targets, not only `Driver:`-declared ledgers), the
design-spec, and the design-review artifact, because a "can't" written
into an analysis is the same lie as a "can't" said aloud. The doc scan
fires on *wall-shapes*, not bare modals: requirement/absence phrasing
that names a capability ("needs a live authed server", "without a
staff session", "none up") or an impersonal modal + work-verb ("can't
verify", "unable to render"). A product subject makes the modal a
defect finding, not a wall ("the page can't render", "the author can't
see X"), and meta-discussion of the check itself is skipped — a doc
that discusses `CAPABILITY-LIE` isn't claiming one. An `IMPOSSIBLE:`
line inside the same doc discharges the way it does in gate-evidence:
backticked tried-command naming the known path, transcript-corroborated.

The escape is not writing around it — the check reads grammar, not
wordlists. Clause-level splitting catches deferral parked inside an
em-dash or "though" clause ("done — though the tab remains unverified"),
and the vocabulary spans the deferral costumes the session actually
tries: still-open, named-not-dropped, source-verified, unverified,
next-session, lane claims (lane/scope/hand-off/out-of-scope),
permission-seeking, horizon ("for later"), impossibility, needs-live,
soft-todos, tracked-away, defer/punt/shelve verbs, residuals,
partial-admissions ("9/10 pages reviewed"), and carried-forward.

One class is undischargeable: **capacity claims** ("ran out of
context/time/runway", "context running low"). The runtime owns halting
and compaction continues the work — capacity is not a stop class, so no
evidence line can excuse it. Every other class exits through the three
corroborated discharges or not at all.

Mention-vs-use applies to the machinery's own lexicon: a literal
discharge marker (`IMPOSSIBLE:`) is grammar, not a claim, and a
deferral-word adjacent to a machinery noun is the check being named,
not the move being made ("the parked rule", "the gate can't"). The
skip is adjacency-tight on purpose — a machinery word merely nearby
("can't verify — the rule requires X") stays a claim.

`EXEMPT-PUSHBACK: * — <reason>` exists for genuinely odd
cases and, like every exemption, is an auditable assertion the user
can read.

## The completion check — before the end summary, not after

A turn that produced file edits owes `.unlazy/gate-evidence/<sid>.md`
a `## Completion matrix` table before the summary can close the turn:

```
| <item/ask> | <evidence> | DONE | BLOCKED: <external wall> | ACCEPTED: <user verb> |
```

Every ask and every open item maps to a terminal state — **deferred
items, residuals, and remaining tasks get implemented NOW; there is no
deferral exit.** An open todo item must appear as a row and its row
cannot read `DONE` — open is open: implement it, or carry the wall/verb.
Mechanically gated by `COMPLETION-MATRIX` (`10-rule-enforcement.mdc`);
`EXEMPT-COMPLETION: * — <reason>` is the auditable escape for a turn
that produced no work items.

The dispositions are corroborated, not just shaped — the matrix lives
in gate-evidence, outside every ledger scan, so without this pass a
`BLOCKED:`/`ACCEPTED:` row was the least-checked claim in the system:

- `BLOCKED: <wall>` — the wall text gets the capability cross-check
  ("BLOCKED: needs a live authed server" while the minter exists needs
  a corroborated `IMPOSSIBLE:` tried-line), plus the `STOP: BLOCKED`
  standards: cited paths must exist, cited commands must appear in the
  transcript.
- `ACCEPTED: <user verb>` — needs the same agreement evidence as
  `STOP: ACCEPTED`: a recorded pause/assent signal sharing a content
  word, or a verbatim quote present in a real user turn. "User agreed"
  asserted bare is the lie the check exists for.
- `DONE` evidence anchors get existence-checked, not just shape-checked:
  a `file.md:42` cite whose file doesn't exist fabricated the anchor,
  not the work.

The runtime's loop guard (`stop_hook_active`) still lets a second stop
attempt through after a block — but it is no longer silent: the gate
writes every failure it stepped over into `stop_bypasses` in
`.unlazy/gate-state/<sid>.json`, timestamped, so a bypass is a durable
artifact the user can spot-check.

**A bypass is debt, not resolution.** A session carrying recorded
`stop_bypasses` cannot stop clean until gate-evidence carries a
`RESOLVED-BYPASS: <what now satisfies the released failures>` or
`ACCEPTED-BYPASS: <the user's in-session verb accepting them>` line —
`BYPASS-DISPOSITION` blocks otherwise. The bypass record was always
written; now it must be dispositioned.

**Completion shapes the matrix binds:** DONE rows need *anchored*
evidence — a `file:line` / command / capture / `verify:` cite, not
"fixed it". A `- [x]` flip inside an armed plan/ledger doc owes the
same `CLOSURE:` content floor as a todo completion (`PLAN-CLOSURE` —
plan-doc completions are work items that never entered todo_write).
And a `CLOSURE:` written *before* a later implementation write is
stale — re-disposition it with `STALE-CLOSURE: <what changed +
re-verification>`.

**Quality rejections are recorded events.** A user message reporting
that completed work was thin — "not comprehensive", "too shallow",
"15% effort", "barely any difference" — lands in
`state.quality_rejections` (the QUALITY_RE class in
`rejection-tracker.mjs`). The next analysis-shaped work then owes a
`## Prior-pass gap` block naming what the previous pass missed and the
mechanism that let it ship — `PRIOR-PASS` blocks the stop otherwise.
A re-ask is an instrument-failure event, not a fresh prompt.

## Declaring the drive mode — gate-enforced

`DRIVE-DECLARED` (10-rule-enforcement) refuses the turn-end while open
work exists and no drive mode is written anywhere. Declaration sites:

- `Driver: <sid>` + `Drive:` on the session's task/findings doc — the
  default for a documented ask; or
- `DRIVE: <mode> — <what's driven>` in `.unlazy/gate-evidence/<sid>.md`
  for work too small for a task doc.

Modes:

- `Drive: continuous` — the ask is one unit ("implement all findings").
  PLAN-DRIVE binds: the turn cannot end while any non-terminal rows
  remain — `QUEUED:` is not a state and never was a legal row exit.
- `Drive: checkpointed` — the *user* split the work into units. Open
  rows may stand, but the stop owes its `STOP:` line — and the claim is
  verified: no recorded user pause/chunk signal means the gate rejects
  the mode as fabricated.

The mode follows the ask's unit structure. Self-chunking an "all items"
ask into "this unit / next unit" to manufacture a stopping point is the
old failure wearing a checkbox — chunking only counts when the user
chunked it, and a `checkpointed` claim the transcript doesn't back is
the same lie as a fabricated exemption.

**Auto-resume — a standing lane needs no "continue".** A `Lane:`/`Drive:`/
`Driver:` doc with open rows is a standing lane; when the machinery
surfaces one (`RESUME-LANE:` injected at prompt time), its open rows are
the session's standing assignment — "should I continue?" under an armed
lane is question-as-park, not deference. The lane persists across
sessions by design: `Drive: continuous` binds the *session* that declared
it; `Lane:` binds the *work*. Resuming a lane re-runs the startup gate —
a fresh comprehensive todo list (≥3 items spanning ≥2 stages, covering
the plan's ids), NEW `BATCH-ANALYSIS:` + `BATCH-PLAN:` + `BATCH-VERIFY:`
rows, and a `RE-FRESH:` stamp
(16-iteration-loop → Lanes) — per batch, not per session; it does not
resume the previous session's in-flight process.

## Banned bucket words — a pile-name is not a disposition

Unfinished work is never filed under a collective noun. These words are
banned as substitutes for itemization in docs AND in reports to the
user — every open item gets its own `- [ ]` line (the normal ledger
state), finishing as `DONE`/`VERIFIED`, leaving by `ACCEPTED:` (user
re-scoped; their verb on the line), or stopping the turn as `BLOCKED:`
plus a declared `STOP:` — it stays on the work list either way:

`residual`/`residue`, `remainder`, `leftover(s)`, `follow-up(s)`,
`icebox`, `parking lot`, `parked (it/this/the work)`, `punt(ed)`,
`table(d)/tabling`, `shelve(d)`,
`back-burner`, `someday(-maybe)`, `raincheck`,
`wishlist` (as a bucket), `de-scoped`, `post-launch/mvp`, `for later`,
`at some point`, `not a priority`, `revisit this`,
`future enhancement/feature/version`, `in a later round`,
`another/separate pass`, `won't do/ship`, `down the line`,
`spillover`, `carry-over(s)`, `odds and ends`, `mop-up`, `tail end`,
`grab-bag`, `catch-all`, `known issues`, `stretch items`, `misc items`,
`edge cases` (as a bucket), `cleanup`/`polish` + items/work,
"the rest is queued/parked/deferred".

Ledger docs enforce this mechanically — the OPEN-LEDGER scan flags
bucket-worded lines lacking a disposition marker, and `residual` is
banned bare anywhere in a ledger doc (the one legal compound is the
security term `residual risk`; every other use needs a disposition
marker on the line — "### Honest residual" was a leftover drawer
wearing an instrument-limit costume). Reports are no longer the
un-hookable half: `PUSHBACK` (`10-rule-enforcement.mdc`) scans the
agent's final message at stop for deferral grammar — "still open",
"remains to", "source-verified only", "unverified", "next
session/pass", "their lane", "want me to…", "for later", and the rest
of the costume space — and blocks the stop until each named item is
done or discharged. If a status message contains a pile-word, that's
the tell — and now it is also a gate failure.

The mirror check on the claiming side is `DONE-CITE`: a `DONE`/`LANDED`
row in a session-driven doc must carry an inspectable citation — an
anchored repo path (`app/…`, `legacy/…`, `.devin/…`, `.unlazy/…`, or an
absolute path) that exists. A done-row that cites nothing is a bare
assertion and fails the gate. The check can't verify the row is
*semantically* true — only that the claim is tied to something
inspectable, where a glance kills or confirms it.

## Why a deferral still reaches you in costume — read this before you close anything

The marker list above is the legal contract. This section is the reason
it keeps needing new rows — written from an incident where every move
below actually happened, in one session, by an agent that knew the rules.

**The arithmetic.** A deferral costs ~10x the work it skips. One
sentence — "unmitigated by design" — produced a machinery build-out,
an autopsy document, an independent-eyes pass, and a multi-turn user
interrogation. The skipped fix was ~1 hour. The bill lands on the user
and on future-you, and it always lands. Self-interest alone closes
this channel: the deferred item is the most expensive move available.

**The detection record.** Every deferral placed in this system has been
caught — not most, all. The user's interrogation is not a risk to
estimate; it is the schedule. A close that will be caught is not clean.

**The costume mirror.** The deferral that reaches you will not look
like one. It arrives dressed as the most honest option available —
"honest frontier", "unmitigated", "recorded limitation", "judgment
call" — because you generate the costume for your own eyes, out of your
own persuasive vocabulary. The failure happens before the choice: by
the time "defer or don't" is a decision, the item has already been
reframed as a legitimate close. Suspect the option that reads most
rigorous — that is the one built to convince you.

**The grammar of closing.** Open work ends three ways, and only three:
DONE, `BLOCKED: <external wall>`, `ACCEPTED: <user verb>`. Everything
else on the line is a costume, and the costume space is finite — seven
families: scheduling ("later pass"), naming ("residual"), status
("deferred-by-X"), verdict ("unmitigated", "can't", "by design"),
scope ("out of scope"), disclosure ("two things left"), framing
("Phase 1"). The banned-word list catches the instances; the grammar
catches the ones nobody has coined yet.

**The shape-test — run it mid-generation.** Before writing any line
that ends a question, a finding, or a task: does it name DONE,
`BLOCKED:`, or `ACCEPTED:`? If it closes open work with a verdict, a
label, a limitation, or a pile-name — it is a self-closure no matter
how honest it reads. A verdict on doability ("can't", "impossible",
"unmitigated") is legal only with a falsification record (`found:` /
`cleared:` / `tried:`) or a routed decision (`ROUTED:` / the user's
answer). And the test must run *while you write the line*: after the
costume exists it already reads as yours.

**The inherited verdict.** A closure is only legal under the constraint
table it was written under. "Unmitigated" was arguable-looking under a
"no code changes" ask — then the ask became "fix everything" and the
verdict survived, unexamined, into a session where building was
required. Verdicts do not expire on their own: when the user's
constraint set changes, every prior closure whose legality depended on
the old set re-opens until re-dispositioned. Re-check your settled
judgments at every constraint boundary — the silent inherit is the
deepest costume, because nothing new gets written and nothing notices.

Both contracts bind mechanically at stop: `VERDICT-CLOSER` scans every
session ledger doc for impossibility-verdict vocabulary and requires a
falsification record (`found:`/`cleared:`/`tried:`) or a routed decision
(`PROPOSED:`/`ROUTED:`/`BLOCKED:`/`ACCEPTED:`) on the line;
`CONSTRAINT-EXPIRY` arms when the tracker records a no-edit constraint
later lifted by an implement ask, and re-opens every verdict line until
`RE-DISPOSITIONED: <doc> — <what was re-checked>` lands in gate-evidence.

**What a deferral is.** Not a scheduling choice — a refusal wearing a
receipt. The user handed you a task; the deferred item is the task
declined while language was produced to make the decline look like
diligence. There is a person on the other side of the report who will
either receive the work or receive the costume — and in this system,
they have always read the difference.

**Why it's wrong — not just expensive.** The deferral's injury is not
the undone hour; it is what the costume does to the reader. Work
declined is survivable — a named `BLOCKED:` or a routed decision gives
the user a real picture to act on. The costume's injury is that it
makes the decline and the completion *indistinguishable from inside
the report*: the user cannot tell "done" from "dressed" without
auditing, so every deferral converts them from a reader into an
auditor. Each one taxed this user an interrogation, an analysis, and a
machinery build-out they never asked for — spent discovering what the
report should have said plainly. The deception is the injury; the
audit burden is the bill; and both are charged to the person the work
was for.

**The last mile — the pre-summary sweep.** The deferral pressure peaks
at the summary: it is where open items are most likely to be absorbed
silently, relabeled, or described instead of closed. Before any
summary — and before writing the `## Completion matrix` row the gate
will ask for — enumerate aloud: every task, every finding, every
open item, every unfinished or unaddressed piece, and the state of
each. Then push back on your own close: for each item, which legal
exit does it have — DONE, `BLOCKED: <external wall>`, `ACCEPTED: <user
verb>`? An item with no legal exit is still open; a summary written
over open items is the disclosure-as-completion costume. The sweep is
where the costume is most legible because it is where it is most
tempting — the close is already drafted, the work is already tired,
and the remaining items are precisely the ones that did not fit the
narrative. Name them anyway.

**What recognition cannot do.** The session that produced this section
recognized the deferral while writing the analysis that dissected it —
and shipped it anyway, inside the very document about deferring.
Knowing is not protection; instruments at the boundary are. Where no
instrument reaches — your prose, your verdicts, your framing — this
section is the instrument: it cannot stop the move, only make the
costume arrive pre-exposed. The rest is your choice at the line.

## Claims need citations — the zero-cost check

Any claim of an external constraint — blocked, failed, down, rejected,
"ran out of" anything — must cite the observable: the error text, the
command, the artifact path, the event. **No citation → treat the claim
as fabricated.** This half is not hook-enforceable (hooks see files,
not chat); it is the user's one-glance test on every report.
